OpenXR Simulator @PROJECT_VERSION@

Launch and inspect OpenXR applications without a headset. This is a development
preview. The app, native MCP connector and Luau runner need neither Python nor Node.

START

Windows: run the installer or extract the ZIP and open bin/simxr_companion.exe.
Linux: extract the archive and run ./install.sh for desktop integration, or open
./bin/simxr_companion directly. Keep the complete package together when moving it.

Choose Add application, select an executable and Launch. New profiles use the
bundled runtime without changing the system default. Connect attaches to an
already-running simulator application. Input replay does not restore game state.

REQUIREMENTS

Both platforms need a Vulkan-capable driver and the system Vulkan loader, even
for Direct3D/OpenGL applications. Run Windows applications without elevation.
Linux packages target Ubuntu 24.04 LTS and use system X11, Vulkan and libcurl
libraries. The optional headset host and OpenXR probes also need libopenxr-loader1.

AUTOMATION

Use Preferences > MCP > Copy MCP configuration in the app. For terminal setup:

  Windows: .\bin\simxr_mcp_connector.exe --print-config --windowless
  Linux:   ./bin/simxr_mcp_connector --print-config --windowless

Merge the output into your client's configuration. Omit --windowless for a visible
app. After moving the package, regenerate its configuration. Install the optional
Python SDK with pip from share/simxr/sdk/python. Use simxr_runner --help for Luau.

UPDATE OR REMOVE

Close the simulator, connector and applications using its runtime before updating
or removing it. Run the new Windows installer or Linux install.sh to upgrade.
For a custom Linux location, pass --prefix /absolute/path on install and uninstall.
Use Windows Settings to uninstall, or run ./install.sh --uninstall on Linux.
Portable installations can be deleted directly. User settings and recordings
are stored separately and preserved.

Releases: https://github.com/Crementif/OpenXR-Simulator/releases
